13:25:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's true, and it's actually well-supported by research—studies have found lice can survive many hours of submersion, which is wild when you think about it. The claim isn't a myth; it holds up scientifically. That said, worth noting a couple of nuances: - "Basically impossible to drown" is close to true for typical bath conditions, but it's not literally impossible under any circumstances. Prolonged, complete submersion for extended periods (like a full day) can eventually kill them—it's just that no bath lasts anywhere near that long. - The folk wisdom sometimes gets oversimplified into "water does nothing at all," which isn't quite right either. Water and combing (especially with conditioner) can still help *mechanically* loosen lice and nits from hair, even if it's not drowning them outright. That's part of why wet-combing is a legitimate treatment method, just not because it suffocates them. So the core of what's passed down is accurate—regular bathing alone won't get rid of an infestation—but it's less "lice have a magic power" and more "they're just really good at closing off their spiracles and gripping hair, and a 10-20 minute bath isn't remotely long enough to overcome that."
